      <section id="what-is-it">
        <h2>What Is Frozen Shoulder? (Adhesive Capsulitis)</h2>
        <p>Frozen shoulder, medically called adhesive capsulitis, happens when the capsule of tissue surrounding your shoulder joint becomes inflamed, thickens, and tightens. This capsule normally allows your shoulder to move freely in almost every direction. When it stiffens, movement becomes painful and progressively more restricted, regardless of how strong your shoulder muscles are.</p>
        <p>According to UK NHS musculoskeletal services, frozen shoulder develops gradually and, left untreated, can take one to three years to fully resolve on its own.<sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup> This is exactly why early physiotherapy matters — the sooner treatment begins, the shorter and less disruptive the recovery tends to be.</p>
        <p>Frozen shoulder is different from a rotator cuff injury or shoulder impingement, both of which usually involve pain during specific movements rather than a broad, all-direction stiffness. A proper physiotherapy assessment helps confirm which condition you're actually dealing with before treatment begins.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="symptoms">
        <h2>Signs &amp; Symptoms You Shouldn't Ignore</h2>
        <p>Frozen shoulder often starts quietly before it becomes hard to ignore. Common symptoms include:</p>
        <ul class="check">
          <li>Shoulder pain that gets worse at night and disrupts sleep</li>
          <li>Gradually reducing range of motion, even without a clear injury</li>
          <li>Difficulty lifting your arm overhead or rotating it outward</li>
          <li>Struggling with everyday tasks like dressing, combing hair, or reaching behind your back</li>
          <li>A feeling that the shoulder is "locked" or "stuck"</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If these symptoms have lasted more than two to three weeks, or if night pain is affecting your sleep, it's worth having a physiotherapist assess your shoulder. Waiting it out rarely makes the condition resolve faster — prolonged immobility tends to make the capsule tighter.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stages">
        <h2>The 3 Stages of Frozen Shoulder</h2>
        <p>Frozen shoulder typically progresses through three distinct stages, and the right physiotherapy approach changes at each one. Treating a "frozen" stage shoulder the same way you'd treat a "freezing" stage shoulder can slow recovery or increase pain — which is why a proper assessment matters before any treatment plan begins.</p>

        <div class="stages">
          <div class="stage-card">
            <span class="num">Stage 1 of 3</span>
            <svg width="56" height="40" viewBox="0 0 56 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 36 A24 24 0 0 1 40 14" fill="none" stroke="#C1792E" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M40 14 A24 24 0 0 1 52 36" fill="none" stroke="#DCD0BC" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
            <h3>Freezing Stage</h3>
            <p>The most painful phase. Pain increases gradually and movement starts becoming limited. Focus: gentle pain relief, protecting the joint, and light pain-free movement. Aggressive stretching here usually backfires.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-card">
            <span class="num">Stage 2 of 3</span>
            <svg width="56" height="40" viewBox="0 0 56 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 36 A24 24 0 0 1 24 12" fill="none" stroke="#C1792E" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M24 12 A24 24 0 0 1 52 36" fill="none" stroke="#DCD0BC" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
            <h3>Frozen Stage</h3>
            <p>Pain may ease, but stiffness becomes severe. Structured manual therapy and graded joint mobilization matter most here — regaining motion gradually without provoking a flare-up.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stage-card">
            <span class="num">Stage 3 of 3</span>
            <svg width="56" height="40" viewBox="0 0 56 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 36 A24 24 0 0 1 48 20" fill="none" stroke="#C1792E" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M48 20 A24 24 0 0 1 52 36" fill="none" stroke="#DCD0BC" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
            <h3>Thawing Stage</h3>
            <p>Movement slowly returns. Physiotherapy shifts to progressive range-of-motion exercises and strengthening the shoulder and scapular muscles to rebuild normal function.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:18px;">Understanding which stage you're in is the first thing our physiotherapist assesses during your home visit — it shapes every decision in your treatment plan.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="causes">
        <h2>Causes &amp; Risk Factors</h2>
        <p>In many cases, frozen shoulder develops without an obvious triggering injury — it's often described as idiopathic. That said, several factors are known to increase risk:</p>
        <ul class="check">
          <li>Diabetes and thyroid disorders</li>
          <li>A previous shoulder injury or surgery</li>
          <li>Prolonged immobility, such as after a fracture or arm sling use</li>
          <li>Age, most commonly between 40 and 60 years</li>
          <li>Being female — frozen shoulder is diagnosed more often in women than men</li>
          <li>A sedentary lifestyle or long periods of poor posture</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Frozen Shoulder and Diabetes</h3>
        <p>Diabetes is one of the best-documented risk factors for frozen shoulder, and diabetes is extremely common across Bangladesh.<sup><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup> In people with diabetes, frozen shoulder tends to develop more often, can take longer to resolve, and more frequently affects both shoulders over time. If you have diabetes and you're noticing shoulder stiffness, it's worth getting it assessed early — early physiotherapy tends to make a meaningful difference in this group.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="treatment">
        <h2>How Physiotherapy Helps — Our Home-Visit Treatment Approach</h2>
        <p>Physiotherapy is widely recognized as the most effective non-surgical way to manage frozen shoulder. Rather than forcing painful movement, our approach focuses on restoring motion gradually and safely, matched to whichever stage you're in.</p>
        <p>A typical home-visit treatment plan includes:</p>
        <ul class="check">
          <li><strong>Manual therapy and capsular stretching</strong> — hands-on techniques to gently mobilize the joint and loosen tight tissue</li>
          <li><strong>Progressive range-of-motion exercises</strong> — starting passive, therapist-assisted, building toward active, independent movement</li>
          <li><strong>Pain modulation techniques</strong> — home-adaptable methods to reduce pain and allow more comfortable movement</li>
          <li><strong>A personalized home exercise program</strong> — specific stretches you continue between visits, adjusted as you progress</li>
          <li><strong>Family and caregiver guidance</strong> — practical advice for household members supporting recovery, especially for elderly patients</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Why This Works Better at Home</h3>
        <p>Practicing shoulder movements in the environment where you actually live — your bedroom, your kitchen, your routine — tends to translate into better real-world recovery than a generic clinic setting. There's also no added stress from traveling through Dhaka traffic before a session, which matters more than people expect when a joint is already inflamed. And because your physiotherapist sees your actual home setup, ergonomic advice can be tailored precisely to your space.</p>
      </section>

