      <section id="exercises">
        <h2>Frozen Shoulder Exercises You Can Do at Home</h2>
        <p>These gentle exercises are commonly recommended for frozen shoulder, but should be introduced gradually and ideally under a physiotherapist's guidance, especially during the painful freezing stage.</p>
        <table>
          <caption>Home exercise guide</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Exercise</th><th scope="col">How to Do It</th><th scope="col">Benefit</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Pendulum Swings</td><td>Lean forward slightly, let your affected arm hang, and gently swing it in small circles</td><td>Relaxes the joint, reduces stiffness without forcing movement</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Towel Stretch</td><td>Hold a towel behind your back with both hands, gently pull upward with the unaffected arm</td><td>Improves internal rotation and flexibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cross-Body Stretch</td><td>Use your other arm to gently pull the affected arm across your chest</td><td>Stretches the back of the shoulder capsule</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Finger Walk</td><td>Face a wall, "walk" your fingers up it as high as comfortable, then slowly lower</td><td>Gradually increases overhead range of motion</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout"><strong>General guide:</strong> 10–15 repetitions, twice a day, always after a light warm-up. Doing these incorrectly — or too aggressively in the wrong stage — can increase pain and slow recovery, which is why professional guidance matters more with frozen shoulder than with most other shoulder problems.</div>
      </section>

      <section id="surgery">
        <h2>Physiotherapy vs. Surgery — Do You Need More Than Physiotherapy?</h2>
        <p>Most frozen shoulder cases respond well to consistent physiotherapy alone, especially when treatment starts early. But physiotherapy isn't always the complete answer for every patient — and it's worth being upfront about that.</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="decision-card ok">
            <h4>Physiotherapy alone is usually enough when:</h4>
            <ul class="check">
              <li>Treatment starts in the freezing or early frozen stage</li>
              <li>You're consistent with home exercises between sessions</li>
              <li>There's steady, measurable improvement in range of motion over several weeks</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="decision-card warn">
            <h4>A doctor referral may be worth discussing when:</h4>
            <ul class="check">
              <li>Stiffness remains severe with minimal improvement after a reasonable course of physiotherapy</li>
              <li>Pain is preventing meaningful participation in therapy</li>
              <li>Your physiotherapist identifies signs that warrant orthopedic evaluation (e.g. manipulation under anaesthesia or hydrodilatation)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>Part of our job as physiotherapists is being upfront with you about your progress. If physiotherapy isn't producing expected improvement within a reasonable timeframe, we'll tell you — and help you understand what a next step with a doctor might look like.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="duration">
        <h2>How Long Does Frozen Shoulder Physiotherapy Take?</h2>
        <p>Left completely untreated, frozen shoulder often resolves on its own — but this natural course commonly takes one to three years.<sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup> Physiotherapy doesn't necessarily shorten the total biological timeline, but it meaningfully reduces pain and disability along the way, and helps you regain functional movement faster than waiting it out.</p>
        <p>The table below reflects typical timeframes for noticeable improvement with active physiotherapy — not full biological resolution of the underlying capsule changes:</p>
        <table>
          <caption>Typical time to noticeable improvement with physiotherapy</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Severity</th><th scope="col">Typical Timeframe</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Mild (early freezing stage)</td><td>Around 6–8 weeks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Moderate (established frozen stage)</td><td>Roughly 3–6 months</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Severe (long-standing stiffness)</td><td>May require longer, structured treatment over several months</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>These are general guides, not guarantees — recovery speed varies a lot from person to person. Your physiotherapist gives a realistic, personalized estimate after your first home assessment.</p>
      </section>

