      <section id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item" open>
          <summary>What is the fastest way to cure frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <div class="a">There's no true "fast cure" for frozen shoulder — it's a condition that resolves gradually even with treatment. Starting physiotherapy early, in the freezing stage, combined with consistent home exercises, gives you the best chance of a shorter, less disruptive recovery.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can physiotherapy fix a frozen shoulder without surgery?</summary>
          <div class="a">Yes, physiotherapy is the most effective non-surgical treatment for frozen shoulder and resolves most cases without any need for surgery, especially when started early.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How many physiotherapy sessions are needed?</summary>
          <div class="a">This depends on the stage and severity of your frozen shoulder. Mild cases may need fewer sessions over 6–8 weeks, while more severe or long-standing cases can require sessions over several months. Your physiotherapist gives a personalized estimate after your first assessment.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is walking good for frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <div class="a">Walking doesn't directly treat frozen shoulder since it doesn't move the shoulder joint, but staying generally active supports overall recovery and circulation. It's not a substitute for shoulder-specific physiotherapy exercises.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does frozen shoulder come back after treatment?</summary>
          <div class="a">Recurrence in the same shoulder is rare once it has fully resolved. It can occasionally develop in the other shoulder later, particularly in people with diabetes. Completing your full treatment plan and home exercise program reduces the risk of lingering stiffness.</div>
        </details>
